From aca4699f1eac7b9fe2508a3d2dac563cd0978cf4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian MacDonald <ian@netstatz.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 18:51:03 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 039/107] fix(server): make single-stream completion and
 journal removal durable

Confirmation-round follow-ups on the download path.

A completed single-stream transfer is now flushed (fdatasync; fsync on
macOS; _commit on Windows) before download_attempt reports success, and
.partial -> final uses rename plus a parent-directory fsync on POSIX
(MoveFileExW with MOVEFILE_WRITE_THROUGH on Windows); the copy fallback
syncs the copy and its directory. A failed flush is reported as a
resumable failure. What remains is the window between the last write and
that flush, the same exposure every append-based resume has at any
partial size, so a guard on only the 416-at-EOF shortcut would not close
it; the content hash is the gate there.

Journal removal is durable: the parent directory is synced after the
unlink on POSIX. On success the journal is first rewritten to the full
size, so a removal lost to power failure (possible on Windows, which has
no directory sync) cannot truncate the finished file. A resurrected
journal can only discard progress, never vouch for missing bytes.

local_failure reports can_resume from the result of normalizing the
partial; the write-failure path, reached only after a successful
normalization, keeps the prefix. Between attempts the resume offset
follows the partial's real size when it shrank, not only when it grew.
Progress callback exceptions in the parallel loop now cancel the attempt
instead of unwinding past live curl handles.

A single-stream resume answered with 200 was checked, not assumed:
libcurl already fails it with CURLE_RANGE_ERROR and leaves the partial
untouched. The new test keeps that as a regression guard.

Assisted-by: claude:opus-5.5
